<h2>Spice demand at a scale most buyers don't expect</h2>
<p>China's position as India's largest single-country market for both dried red chilli and cumin often surprises buyers who associate Indian spice exports mainly with the Middle East. The scale of Chinese food-processing and culinary demand, combined with India's production capacity, has made this one of our most significant regional relationships by value.</p>

<h2>Registration comes before trade</h2>
<p>The defining practical feature of exporting into this region is facility-level product registration — China's Decree 248 requires GACC registration for many food categories before goods can clear customs, a process that needs to start well ahead of a first shipment rather than during it.</p>

<h2>Standards are set independently</h2>
<p>China sets its own residue and quality standards, separate from the EU or US. A product cleared for one market isn't automatically cleared for another — tell us your specific destination early so we test and document against the right requirement.</p>
